    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4291",
      "problem": "Let $A(-2,1)$ and $B(2,3).$ Find $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Our vector is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252323/assets/76c98067c9780e9c903dda8657b67a2db473ecd8ef24e53e2262ba2f46a4293a.png)\n\nWe have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=-2i+j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=2i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j)-(-2i+j) \\\\  & =4i+2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
